#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110326 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110603 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69089475-1b80-5230-941c-433dd6cd564c" :knowledge-point/title "Equations Containing a Variable in the Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "deda4937-0332-5584-b6a1-fa840e750be1" :question/math-academy-id "e-14138" :question/problem "Find the solution to $\\dfrac {1} {x-2} = 5 .$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=2$ cannot be a solution. This is because if $x=2,$ then the rational term would be $\\dfrac{1}{0},$ which is undefined because it has a zero denominator.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by the denominator $(x-2)$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-2} & =5 \\\\ (x-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2} & =(x-2)\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\cancel{(x-2)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{x-2}} & =5x-10 \\\\ 1 & =5x-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =5x-10 \\\\ 1+10 & =5x-10+10 \\\\ 11 & =5x \\\\ \\frac{11}{5} & =\\frac{5x}{5} \\\\ \\frac{11}{5} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=\\dfrac{11}{5}$ is different from $x=2$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=\\dfrac{11}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110327 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110604 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb34628f-b5ab-5ce7-8d92-576f5adaf54d" :knowledge-point/title "Equations Containing Variables in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "9640178a-cada-5fd6-8622-9bef515ed443" :question/math-academy-id "e-14141" :question/problem "Solve $\\dfrac {3x} {x+1} = 2 .$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by the denominator $(x+1)$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{x+1} & =2 \\\\ (x+1)\\cdot \\frac{3x}{x+1} & =(x+1)\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\cancel{(x+1)}\\cdot \\frac{3x}{\\cancel{x+1}} & =2x+2 \\\\ 3x & =2x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =2x+2 \\\\ 3x-2x & =2x+2-2x \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  $x = 2$ is different from $x=-1$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110328 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa33473c-6431-59c9-a96c-1e99fa959058" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where Leading Coefficient in the Denominator Is Not One"}] :question/id #uuid "d4adcae2-06f8-5d3d-ac48-1aca6c8a0c29" :question/math-academy-id "e-14144" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac {1} {2x+1} = 2 .$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator $2x+1$ cannot be zero, since division by $0$ is undefined. Therefore we cannot have $2x+1=0,$ which means $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ cannot be a solution.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by the denominator $(2x+1)$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2x+1} & =2 \\\\ (2x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2x+1} & =(2x+1)\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\cancel{(2x+1)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{2x+1}} & =4x+2 \\\\ 1 & =4x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =4x+2 \\\\ 1-2 & =4x+2-2 \\\\ -1 & =4x \\\\ \\frac{-1}{4} & =\\frac{4x}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  $x = -\\dfrac{1}{4}$ is different from $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=-\\dfrac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110603 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69089475-1b80-5230-941c-433dd6cd564c" :knowledge-point/title "Equations Containing a Variable in the Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "115c7e88-e294-563e-b7fc-d6e13af09301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491490} {:answer/id #uuid "277901e2-2e4e-51d3-8f8f-52c3cdfae5fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491536} {:answer/id #uuid "d36c7eae-ad1c-5d76-b08d-c073bf3ec6a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491812} {:answer/id #uuid "de1afd07-bc38-5faa-8e54-d5d9a34c84f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491832} {:answer/id #uuid "f8eddd8a-994f-5375-bc87-42d9ed33ef54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "115c7e88-e294-563e-b7fc-d6e13af09301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491490} :answer-field/id #uuid "840b454c-eb1c-53de-b5cd-6eaf38c6d59c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "125745c2-3a6a-569e-8401-2476871f47c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-102158" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{27}{x}=9.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution. This is because if $x=0,$ then the rational term would be $\\frac{27}{0},$ which is undefined because it has a zero denominator.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by the denominator $x$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{27}{x} & =9 \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{27}{x} & =x\\cdot 9 \\\\ \\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{27}{\\cancel{x}} & =9x \\\\ 27 & =9x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27 & =9x \\\\ \\frac{27}{9} & =\\frac{9x}{9} \\\\ 3 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=3$ is different from $x=0$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110603 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69089475-1b80-5230-941c-433dd6cd564c" :knowledge-point/title "Equations Containing a Variable in the Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0612a474-15cf-5ca3-9ef5-cba54ea735a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0612a474-15cf-5ca3-9ef5-cba54ea735a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491464} :answer-field/id #uuid "83b5d553-a11d-5018-8c96-6283451c3522" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f04aac8-e6e7-5764-9921-97e34f5f1880" :question/math-academy-id "q-212105" :question/problem "The solution to $\\frac{12}{x}=3$ is$\\,\\,x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution. This is because if $x=0,$ then the rational term would be $\\frac{12}{0},$ which is undefined because it has a zero denominator.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by the denominator $x$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{x} & =3 \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{12}{x} & =x\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{12}{\\cancel{x}} & =3x \\\\ 12 & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =3x \\\\ \\frac{12}{3} & =\\frac{3x}{3} \\\\ 4 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=4$ is different from $x=0$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110603 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69089475-1b80-5230-941c-433dd6cd564c" :knowledge-point/title "Equations Containing a Variable in the Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "536841c1-edde-5de3-b457-ba28e7ada21a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "536841c1-edde-5de3-b457-ba28e7ada21a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491613} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0cec57d-17f0-55fa-b6d8-27606e0ec9c4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f69af0e1-99d0-5dff-a9e0-4071bc340c80" :question/math-academy-id "q-216627" :question/problem "The solution to $\\frac{16}{x}=8$ is$\\,\\,x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution. This is because if $x=0,$ then the rational term would be $\\frac{16}{0},$ which is undefined because it has a zero denominator.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by the denominator $x$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16}{x} & =8 \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{16}{x} & =x\\cdot 8 \\\\ \\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{16}{\\cancel{x}} & =8x \\\\ 16 & =8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 & =8x \\\\ \\frac{16}{8} & =\\frac{8x}{8} \\\\ 2 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=2$ is different from $x=0$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492027 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110603 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69089475-1b80-5230-941c-433dd6cd564c" :knowledge-point/title "Equations Containing a Variable in the Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2aed3d1-46f3-505c-b8d3-44a4e346f810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2aed3d1-46f3-505c-b8d3-44a4e346f810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491739} :answer-field/id #uuid "731c1352-0946-5779-ab4d-5a613f532d8c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74bd71aa-77f2-5627-a97e-2a16e71261b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-216629" :question/problem "The solution to $14=\\frac{7}{x}$ is$\\,\\,x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution. This is because if $x=0,$ then the rational term would be $\\frac{7}{0},$ which is undefined because it has a zero denominator.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by the denominator $x$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =\\frac{7}{x} \\\\ x\\cdot 14 & =x\\cdot \\frac{7}{x} \\\\ x\\cdot 14 & =\\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{7}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\ 14x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14x & =7 \\\\ \\frac{14x}{14} & =\\frac{7}{14} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is different from $x=0$, we can accept it as a solution.  Thus, the solution is $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110603 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69089475-1b80-5230-941c-433dd6cd564c" :knowledge-point/title "Equations Containing a Variable in the Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6fdee64-02fa-5fb6-a953-624fe74e1397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6fdee64-02fa-5fb6-a953-624fe74e1397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491851} :answer-field/id #uuid "9efd42be-572d-5f0b-beba-3622a80db47b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6a33051-a015-5020-a25d-850890a866df" :question/math-academy-id "q-216636" :question/problem "The solution to $\\frac{9}{2x}=3$ is$\\,\\,x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution. This is because if $x=0,$ then the rational term would be $\\frac{9}{2\\cdot 0},$ which is undefined because it has a zero denominator.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by $(2x)$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2x} & =3 \\\\ 2x\\cdot \\frac{9}{2x} & =3\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\cancel{2x}\\cdot \\frac{9}{\\cancel{2x}} & =6x \\\\ 9 & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =6x \\\\ \\frac{9}{6} & =\\frac{6x}{6} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is different from $x=0$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110604 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb34628f-b5ab-5ce7-8d92-576f5adaf54d" :knowledge-point/title "Equations Containing Variables in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2ac5098-1567-542e-b63d-4fd4b7549caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2ac5098-1567-542e-b63d-4fd4b7549caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491869} :answer-field/id #uuid "65a4dc5e-944d-52ba-be6f-ed10a5b50985" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf8fa259-5b82-5b77-ac27-d9afdcd671e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-216640" :question/problem "The solution to $\\frac{6x}{x+4}=3$ is$\\,\\,x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=-4$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by the denominator $(x+4)$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6x}{x+4} & =3 \\\\ (x+4)\\cdot \\frac{6x}{x+4} & =(x+4)\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\cancel{(x+4)}\\cdot \\frac{6x}{\\cancel{x+4}} & =(x+4)\\cdot 3 \\\\ 6x & =3x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & =3x+12 \\\\ 6x-3x & =3x+12-3x \\\\ 3x & =12 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{12}{3} \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=4$ is different from $x=-4$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110604 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb34628f-b5ab-5ce7-8d92-576f5adaf54d" :knowledge-point/title "Equations Containing Variables in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b98fa3e-fd9e-56d8-962b-e9da30134989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b98fa3e-fd9e-56d8-962b-e9da30134989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491509} :answer-field/id #uuid "877ec549-50d2-55e5-8078-eac222f02432" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "695f0f45-ca26-5a8e-af61-5e5eccb763aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-216650" :question/problem "The solution to $2=\\frac{6x}{x+10}$ is$\\,\\,x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=-10$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by the denominator $(x+10)$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{6x}{x+10} \\\\ (x+10)\\cdot 2 & =(x+10)\\cdot \\frac{6x}{x+10} \\\\ (x+10)\\cdot 2 & =\\cancel{(x+10)}\\cdot \\frac{6x}{\\cancel{x+10}} \\\\ 2x+20 & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+20 & =6x \\\\ 2x+20-2x & =6x-2x \\\\ 20 & =4x \\\\ \\frac{20}{4} & =\\frac{4x}{4} \\\\ 5 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  $x=5$ is different from $x=-10$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa33473c-6431-59c9-a96c-1e99fa959058" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where Leading Coefficient in the Denominator Is Not One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "106c0d02-6d8e-5eea-9dbf-5e7592550699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "106c0d02-6d8e-5eea-9dbf-5e7592550699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491486} :answer-field/id #uuid "49677076-165e-533f-9555-3c5ab6fe40b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b74041d-b56d-586f-9721-4bae2781f6fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-216726" :question/problem "The solution to $\\frac{7x+4}{2x-1}=5$ is$\\,\\,x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by the denominator $(2x-1)$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7x+4}{2x-1} & =5 \\\\ (2x-1)\\cdot \\frac{7x+4}{2x-1} & =(2x-1)\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\cancel{(2x-1)}\\cdot \\frac{7x+4}{\\cancel{2x-1}} & =(2x-1)\\cdot 5 \\\\ 7x+4 & =10x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x+4 & =10x-5 \\\\ 7x+4+5 & =10x-5+5 \\\\ 7x+9 & =10x \\\\ 7x+9-7x & =10x-7x \\\\ 9 & =3x \\\\ \\frac{9}{3} & =\\frac{3x}{3} \\\\ 3 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=3$ is different from $x=\\frac{1}{2}$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492047 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa33473c-6431-59c9-a96c-1e99fa959058" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where Leading Coefficient in the Denominator Is Not One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05781782-c6f6-533d-a210-0a8e49502425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05781782-c6f6-533d-a210-0a8e49502425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491459} :answer-field/id #uuid "efab8bb6-8d0f-5b1a-9b70-66510c02bde6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2a4b507-db6c-548d-8dc8-8ead746b1a3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-216906" :question/problem "The solution to $\\frac{8}{3x-2}=2$ is$\\,\\,x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=\\frac{2}{3}$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined.  \n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by the denominator $(3x-2)$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3x-2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{8(3x-2)}{(3x-2)} & =2(3x-2) \\\\ \\frac{8\\cancel{(3x-2)}}{\\cancel{(3x-2)}} & =2(3x-2) \\\\ 8 & =6x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =6x-4 \\\\ 8+4 & =6x-4+4 \\\\ 12 & =6x \\\\ \\frac{12}{6} & =\\frac{6x}{6} \\\\ 2 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  $x=2$ is different from $x=\\frac{2}{3}$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=2.$"}]}
